     "content": "### 1. Breakdown and Comparison\n\nAt first glance, comparing annualized averages ($552,000/yr vs. $526,000/yr) makes the offer look like a modest bump. However, **Google does not vest new-hire RSUs evenly at 25% per year.**\n\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (commonly 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%):\n\n| Compensation Element | Meta (Current IC6) | Google Offer (L6) \u2014 Year 1 *(33% vest)* | Google Offer \u2014 4-Yr Flat Average |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 |\n| **Equity (RSUs)** | ~$250,000 ($200k next 12m) | **$356,400** (33% of $1.08M) | $270,000/yr |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \u2014 | $0 | $0 |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$552,000** |\n\n#### Key Insights:\n* **The Equity is Strong:** A **$1.08M** 4-year grant is in the upper quartile (75th+ percentile) for a Google L6 PM. Due to front-loading, your **Year 1 and Year 2 compensation will be ~$638,000+**.\n* **The Base is a Slight Pay Cut:** Google offered $235,000 against a listed range of $183,000\u2013$271,000, which is below your current $240,000 base.\n* **The Equity Cliff in Years 3\u20134:** In Years 3 and 4, the initial grant drops to 22% ($237.6k) and 12% ($129.6k). Google relies on annual performance refreshes to fill this gap, but as your recruiter noted, refreshes depend on performance ratings and are not guaranteed.\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** You are leaving unvested equity and upcoming bonus payouts at Meta without an upfront buffer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Verdict: Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away.** **You should negotiate.**\n\nYou have ideal negotiation leverage:\n1. You were passively recruited and are happy in your current role.\n2. Google\u2019s base salary is lower than what you already make.\n3. They have given you $0 in sign-on bonus to bridge transition costs.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For & Concrete Numbers\n\nBecause your $1.08M equity grant is already near the top of the L6 band, your easiest and highest-probability wins will come from **Base Salary** and a **Sign-on Bonus**.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter-Ask | Target Settlement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** *(Within the $271k band max)* | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n| **RSU Grant** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000** *(or hold firm if they push on cash)* | $1,080,000 \u2013 $1,150,000 |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Conversation\n\nSend an email or set up a brief call with your recruiter using the framework below:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the scope of the role.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am performing well at Meta, compensation is a major factor in making a move. Looking at the details:*\n> \n> 1. *My current base is $240,000, so $235,000 represents a step backward in guaranteed cash. Given the posted range goes up to $271,000, I\u2019d like to see the base at **$255,000**.*\n> 2. *Leaving Meta means walking away from upcoming bonus and RSU vesting milestones. To offset that transition, I\u2019m looking for a sign-on bonus of **$85,000**.*\n> 3. *On equity, if we can adjust the RSU grant to **$1.2M**, this would make the decision very straightforward for me to sign.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*\n\n### Summary\n* **Base:** Push to $250k+ so your cash pay increases.\n* **Sign-on:** Ask for $75k\u2013$100k; Google routinely uses sign-ons to close senior PM hires from Meta.\n* **RSUs:** Ask for $1.2M, but recognize that even at $1.08M, your Year 1 cash flow will be significantly higher than your current setup due to Google's front-loaded vesting.",
